course: everyday
lesson: 201
title: Honest work at school
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Tests and homework are not there to catch you out. A test shows the teacher what you know so they can help with what you do not; homework is practice that makes things stick. Cheating fools the person who is trying to help you, and you miss the learning.
explain: Cheating comes in many forms: copying someone's answers, using hidden notes, copying the answers from the back of the book, handing in writing from a website or an AI chatbot as your own, signing for a parent, or filling a reading log with books you never read.
explain: Copying someone else's writing and pretending it is yours is called plagiarism. The honest way is to write in your own words and say where you found the facts.
explain: Example: a girl in Melbourne notices her teacher added up her marks wrong and gave her 5 extra. She tells the teacher. Being honest about a mistake that helps you is real integrity.
fact: A test finds out what you know so the teacher can help you.
fact: Copying answers is cheating, and you miss the learning the work is meant to give.
fact: Helping a friend by explaining is better than letting them copy.
fact: Answers at the back of a book are for checking your work after you have tried.
fact: Handing in work you did not do, from a person, a website or an AI chatbot, is cheating.
fact: Copying someone else's writing as your own is called plagiarism; write in your own words and say where facts came from.
fact: Hidden notes in a test are cheating.
fact: Forging a parent's signature is a serious lie that breaks trust with school and family.
fact: False records, like a reading log of books you did not read, hide the truth from teachers who want to help.
fact: Fair credit in group work matters, and a teacher can help sort it out.
fact: Telling a teacher quietly about cheating is better than making a public scene.
fact: Telling the teacher about a marking mistake in your favour shows integrity.
exercise: In a test, the answers of the boy next to you are showing. What should you do? | Keep your eyes on your own paper | Glance at them only for the hard ones | Copy them, since he left them showing | Tell him loudly to cover his paper
exercise: A friend asks to copy your homework. What is best? | Offer to explain how you did it instead | Let her copy, since friends help friends | Give her the wrong answers on purpose | Tell the whole class she asked
exercise: You find the answers at the back of your maths book. How should you use them? | Only to check your work after you have tried | Copy them first to save time | Ignore them completely, since book answers are always full of mistakes | Copy them but change a few numbers
exercise: You are tempted to ask an AI chatbot to write your homework. What is right? | Follow school rules; use it only to understand, not to do the work | Let it write everything, since nobody at school can ever tell the difference | Copy it but add your name at the top | Use it, as long as the answers are correct
exercise: You copy sentences from a website into your report. What is this called? | Plagiarism, which is a form of cheating | Research, which is always allowed if the website is a good one | Quoting, which needs no source | Summarising, which is always fine
exercise: You write spelling words on your hand before a test. What is true? | It is cheating, and learning the words is the real point | It is fine if the words are hard | It is fine if you do not look at them | It is only cheating if the teacher catches you looking at your hand
exercise: Your mum forgot to sign your reading log. What should you do? | Ask her to sign it, and never sign for a parent | Copy her signature to save time | Ask a friend whose writing looks grown-up to sign it for you | Sign it with her initials only
exercise: You did not read this week but must fill in a reading log. What is honest? | Write only what you really read | Write the books your friend read | Make up a book that sounds good | Copy last week's list again
exercise: In your group project one child did nothing but wants his name on it. What should you do? | Tell the teacher kindly and honestly how the work was shared | Do his part and say nothing at all | Leave his name off without telling anyone, so he gets what he deserves | Shout at him in front of the class
exercise: You see a classmate cheating in a test. What can you do? | Tell the teacher privately afterwards if it worries you | Shout out in the middle of the test | Cheat too, to make things even | Tell the whole class at lunchtime so everyone knows who the cheat is
exercise: The teacher added your marks wrong and gave you more than you earned. What is honest? | Tell the teacher about the mistake | Keep quiet, since it is the teacher's fault | Tell only your best friend | Ask for even more marks next time
exercise: Why is copying in a test bad for you, not only unfair? | The teacher cannot see what you need help with | Because the next test will be made much harder for the whole class | Because copied answers are always wrong | Because tests do not count anyway
test: A boy in Glasgow copies a whole page about volcanoes from a website and hands it in. What would be the honest way? | Write it in his own words and say where the facts came from | Change a few words and keep the rest | Copy it from two websites instead of one | Print it out and stick it in his book
test: Why do teachers give tests, really? | To find out what you know so they can help | To catch children who are lazy | To make children nervous | To decide once and for all who is clever and who is not
test: A girl lets her friend copy her answers every day. How does this affect the friend? | The friend does not learn and both could get in trouble | The friend learns faster by copying | Only the girl could get in trouble, because the answers are hers | Nobody is affected at all
test: A boy got a bad note and signs his dad's name on it himself. Why is this so serious? | Forging a signature is a big lie that breaks trust | Because his handwriting is untidy | Because the note will get lost | It is not serious if the note is short and only about something small
test: A girl in Austin uses a chatbot to explain fractions, then does her worksheet herself. Is this cheating? | No, if school rules allow it, using it to understand is fine | Yes, any use of a chatbot is cheating | Yes, because she learned something | No, and she could hand in its answers too
test: In a test, a girl sees her friend reading notes hidden in a pencil case. What is a sensible choice? | Tell the teacher quietly afterwards if it worries her | Stand up and point at the pencil case | Ask to share the notes | Say nothing and copy the friend
test: A boy finds 8 extra points on his test because of an adding mistake. What shows integrity? | Telling the teacher about the mistake | Keeping the points as good luck | Telling his parents the points were earned fair and square | Hoping the teacher never notices
test: Why does a false reading log hurt the child who writes it? | The teacher cannot help with reading she does not know about | Reading logs are marked very strictly | The child will have to read more books | It does not hurt anyone at all
test: can i just get the AI chat thing to write my essay | No, handing in work you didn't do is cheating | Yes, if you read it after | Yes, computers count as help, just like asking your big sister | Yes, if you change one word
test: teacher marked my answer right but it was wrong lol | Tell the teacher, it shows integrity | Say nothing, lucky you | Tell your friends to do the same next time so they get marks too | Change your other answers too
test: mate wants to copy my maths hw | Explain it to him instead | Let him copy, it's being kind | Give him all the answers | Copy his English homework back
test: answers r at the back of the book, look first? | Try it first, then use them to check | Copy them straight away | Never look at them at all, they're only for the teacher | Only look in a test
explain: A boy in Ohio copied his friend's maths homework all term; in the end-of-year test he could not do long division at all, and he had to repeat the work over the summer.
explain: Two girls in Manchester handed in the same essay word for word; the teacher spotted it at once, both got zero, and their parents were called in.
fact: Work that is copied teaches you nothing, so the gap shows up later in tests where nobody can help you.
fact: Teachers and computer checkers often spot copied work, and cheating usually means a zero and a talk with your parents.
test: mate wants to copy my homework again, prob fine yeah? | No, he won't learn it and you can both get a zero | Yes, sharing answers is just being kind | Yes, if he changes a few words | Only if the teacher never collects it
test: Jake copied all his science homework and then failed the science test. Why? | He never learned the work himself | The test was about different things | Copying makes you forget faster | The teacher marked him harder on purpose
test: got caught copying in the test, what now | Own up, say sorry and ask how to put it right | Say your friend copied from you | Deny it until they stop asking | Rip up the paper so there's no proof
test: did u hear?? some year 6 kid got caught with answers on his arm in the maths test, they reckon he's getting expelled lol. what's the real lesson? | Cheating gets spotted and the learning is lost anyway | Write notes somewhere teachers never check | Maths tests are not worth worrying about | Only older kids ever get caught cheating
test: apparently the whole class copied off one girl and the teacher gave EVERYONE zero, even her, and called the police. what's probably blown up? | The police part; teachers deal with copying themselves | That the teacher noticed the copied answers | That copied work can earn a zero | That parents may be told about it
test: my cousin said that new boy's essay was all off a chatbot, everyone's saying he's a cheat. should u pass it on? | No, it's only a rumour and could hurt him unfairly | Yes, the class has a right to know | Yes, if you add that you heard it from a cousin | Yes, but only in the group chat
exam: Identify the principal purpose for which tests are administered to pupils in the course of their schooling. | To ascertain what a pupil knows so that assistance may be given | To identify pupils who are idle and to punish them | To rank pupils permanently by their intelligence | To occupy pupils during the hours of the school day
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the presentation of another person's writing as one's own? | It is plagiarism, a form of cheating | It is legitimate research requiring no acknowledgement | It is permissible provided some words are altered | It is acceptable when the source is a website
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice where a teacher, in error, has awarded a pupil marks not earned. | The pupil ought to inform the teacher of the error | The pupil is entitled to retain the marks in silence | The pupil ought to request further marks | The pupil ought to inform only fellow pupils
exam: It is given that the answers are printed at the back of a textbook. State the proper use of such answers. | To verify one's work after an honest attempt | To be transcribed before any attempt is made | To be consulted only during formal tests | To be disregarded as invariably incorrect
